From 94da5a0696a646b59d858e8f2420f41c1bc27c4b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivan Oparin <oparin@me.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 14:53:35 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] BUG-260902-3ru6vw: admit extension keys by the reverse-DNS
 grammar alone

hasForbiddenConfigName refused any extension key, and any nested extension
object key at any depth, whose dot- or dash-separated part was one of secret,
token, password, credential, auth, environment, env or endpoint. The pinned
v0.5.0 specification states no such rule.

SPEC.md:345-347 defines an extension key as "3-253 lowercase ASCII characters,
contains at least one dot, and has dot-separated labels matching
[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,62}" and reserves no label. SPEC.md:347-349 constrains an
ExtensionValue object only as "string-keyed object with maximum nesting depth
4", so the nested-key arm had no source at all.

The rule also failed as secret detection, which is what its label list implied:
it inspected zero values, so com.example.deploy = "AKIA..." passed while
works.relux.env-tools, com.example.auth-manager and io.example.endpoint-list --
namespaces this organisation owns -- were refused. The three clauses Section 6
does state are value or field-declaration rules and are enforced where they
apply: SPEC.md:2596-2597 by the closed schema a backend implementation version
registers, SPEC.md:2562-2563 by the closed table shape decodeStrict decodes
with DisallowUnknownFields, and SPEC.md:2344-2345 explicitly permits a config
field to name a machine-local environment variable or credential profile.

Remove the blacklist and both call sites, and remove the two refusal cases that
pinned it rather than the rule itself. Add extension_key_admission_test.go,
which drives the production Load entry: legitimate reverse-DNS keys carrying
every previously blacklisted label are admitted and round-trip, nested keys
named endpoint/token/password are preserved as data, the grammar, the 253-byte
key bound and the depth-4 bound stay negative-tested, and the two retained
value/field-declaration gates are pinned.

Record the finding in LOGBOOK.md: the failure shape worth keeping is that a
refusal test pinned an invented constraint through a review loop, so reviewing
a refusal now means quoting its declaring specification line first.
